    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. one concern i have is the potential for \"dark corners\" in the archive—like if we get a bunch of miscellaneous, uncatalogued papers or items that don't quite fit a standard subject matter. how do these systems handle that kind of disorganized or messy overflow? does one deal better with the chaos of unclassified items?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The DDC system handles disorganized items more effectively by permitting a \"miscellaneous\" section (such as 500s for general knowledge or 900s for local history) or by allowing physical grouping under a broad leading number without strict subject coding. In contrast, LCC often requires a specific series letter for a known subject, which can make assigning vague or unfamiliar manuscript fragments less intuitive without adding significant detail to authority files. Therefore, for truly chaotic collections where items lack definable subject matter, the numerical flexibility of DDC allows for easier temporal or thematic sorting, while LCC demands more rigorous intellectual sorting to fit its structured alphanumeric logic.",
